  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday-Friday...Little change is forecast as low level Atlantic \nridge axis remains nearly stationary across south central FL.\nAnd aloft, we remain sandwiched between a pair of mid level highs\nwith weak troughing over the NE Gulf/FL panhandle. Light sfc winds\nin vicinity of the ridge axis will allow the Atlc sea breeze to\nform and push inland sparking scattered to numerous showers and\nlightning storms both aftns and evenings. A S/SW steering flow\nwill bring some of the storms back across the coast esp this\nevening. Sufficient moisture, strong heating and boundary \ncollisions will produce rain chances of 50-70%. Primary threats\nwill continue to be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds up to\n55 mph and heavy rain. Localized amounts around 3 inches will be\npossible, causing temporary flooding of urban and poor drainage\nareas. If threatening weather approaches, seek shelter in an \nenclosed building or automobile. Picnic shelters and screened \nporches may keep you dry but offer no protection from the dangers \nof lightning.\n\nPrior to storms, temperatures will be seasonably hot and humid in\nthe low to mid 90s with heat indices 100-106.\n\nSat-Wed...The weak troughing over the NE Gulf will begin to fill \nthis weekend allowing the ridge aloft over the SW Atlc to nose in\nover FL. Gradually, the 2 upper ridges will merge over the SE U.S.\nand raise heights over FL next week. This will allow the low \nlevel ridge axis to lift north of our area resulting in onshore \nflow becoming dominant in the low and mid levels. Scattered storms\nare forecast each day pushing quickly inland with the Atlc sea \nbreeze, eventually colliding with the Gulf breeze near Orlando or\npoints west late in the day. Immediate coastal communities will \nsee lower rain chances and also warmer overnight lows due to the \npersistent onshore flow, likely holding in the upper 70s/lower \n80s there. Expect seasonably hot and humid daytime conditions to \npersist as we welcome September.",
